1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,684
Anyone on the internet can send a request to your API. So how does it know who is asking?

2
00:00:06,234 --> 00:00:10,970
Without an answer, anyone could read every order, and anyone could delete your products.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,328
Today you learn the answer in four ideas: authentication,

4
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:16,731
which is who you are.

5
00:00:16,731 --> 00:00:17,666
Authorization,

6
00:00:17,666 --> 00:00:19,336
which is what you may do.

7
00:00:19,336 --> 00:00:19,804
Tokens,

8
00:00:19,804 --> 00:00:21,942
which prove it on every request.

9
00:00:21,942 --> 00:00:22,744
And secrets,

10
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:24,213
which must never leak.

11
00:00:24,763 --> 00:00:29,149
And you will meet two numbers every dev ops engineer reads: four oh one,

12
00:00:29,149 --> 00:00:30,246
and four oh three.

13
00:00:32,296 --> 00:00:35,411
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

14
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:36,578
Start it.

15
00:00:36,578 --> 00:00:38,638
Type: dot slash start dot S H.

16
00:00:38,638 --> 00:00:42,275
This version reads its passwords from a secrets file.

17
00:00:42,825 --> 00:00:45,806
Authentication means proving who you are.

18
00:00:45,806 --> 00:00:47,332
Try to log in as Bob,

19
00:00:47,332 --> 00:00:49,076
with the wrong password.

20
00:00:49,076 --> 00:00:49,876
Type: curl,

21
00:00:49,876 --> 00:00:50,530
dash s i,

22
00:00:50,530 --> 00:00:51,621
dash capital X,

23
00:00:51,621 --> 00:00:51,984
post,

24
00:00:51,984 --> 00:00:52,493
dash d,

25
00:00:52,493 --> 00:00:54,601
the jay son in single quotes,

26
00:00:54,601 --> 00:00:55,836
then the address,

27
00:00:55,836 --> 00:00:57,508
slash API slash log in,

28
00:00:57,508 --> 00:00:57,872
pipe,

29
00:00:57,872 --> 00:00:58,235
head,

30
00:00:58,235 --> 00:00:58,889
dash one.

31
00:01:00,439 --> 00:01:01,210
Four oh one.

32
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:02,044
Unauthorized.

33
00:01:02,044 --> 00:01:04,420
The server does not know who you are.

34
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,511
Despite the name,

35
00:01:05,511 --> 00:01:07,309
it means: not authenticated.

36
00:01:07,859 --> 00:01:10,070
Now log in with the right password.

37
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,459
Type the same command,

38
00:01:11,459 --> 00:01:13,039
with Bob's real password,

39
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:14,365
and without the head.

40
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:17,938
Success.

41
00:01:17,938 --> 00:01:21,273
The server gives you a token: a long random string.

42
00:01:21,273 --> 00:01:23,562
It also says your role is customer,

43
00:01:23,562 --> 00:01:25,458
and the token lasts one hour.

44
00:01:26,008 --> 00:01:27,371
The token is your proof.

45
00:01:27,371 --> 00:01:29,133
You send it with every request,

46
00:01:29,133 --> 00:01:31,235
so you never send the password again.

47
00:01:33,285 --> 00:01:37,210
Same shop, new session. Go in. Type: CD, cloud cart.

48
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,811
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

49
00:01:41,361 --> 00:01:42,770
Orders are private.

50
00:01:42,770 --> 00:01:44,771
Ask for them with no token.

51
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:45,587
Type: curl,

52
00:01:45,587 --> 00:01:46,254
dash s i,

53
00:01:46,254 --> 00:01:47,143
the address,

54
00:01:47,143 --> 00:01:48,848
slash API slash orders,

55
00:01:48,848 --> 00:01:49,219
pipe,

56
00:01:49,219 --> 00:01:49,590
head,

57
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:50,257
dash one.

58
00:01:50,807 --> 00:01:53,367
Four oh one again. No token, no entry.

59
00:01:53,917 --> 00:01:54,964
Log in as Bob,

60
00:01:54,964 --> 00:01:57,433
and keep the token in a variable.

61
00:01:57,433 --> 00:01:58,855
Type: token equals,

62
00:01:58,855 --> 00:01:59,379
dollar,

63
00:01:59,379 --> 00:01:59,977
bracket,

64
00:01:59,977 --> 00:02:01,399
the log in command,

65
00:02:01,399 --> 00:02:01,773
pipe,

66
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:02,072
J Q,

67
00:02:02,072 --> 00:02:02,596
dash R,

68
00:02:02,596 --> 00:02:03,344
dot token,

69
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:04,392
close bracket.

70
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:09,071
Now send the token in a header.

71
00:02:09,071 --> 00:02:11,475
The header is called authorization,

72
00:02:11,475 --> 00:02:14,360
and its value starts with the word Bearer.

73
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:15,115
Type: curl,

74
00:02:15,115 --> 00:02:15,596
dash s,

75
00:02:15,596 --> 00:02:16,627
dash capital H,

76
00:02:16,627 --> 00:02:17,039
quote,

77
00:02:17,039 --> 00:02:18,000
authorization,

78
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:18,412
colon,

79
00:02:18,412 --> 00:02:18,893
Bearer,

80
00:02:18,893 --> 00:02:19,305
space,

81
00:02:19,305 --> 00:02:20,198
dollar token,

82
00:02:20,198 --> 00:02:20,610
quote,

83
00:02:20,610 --> 00:02:22,259
then the orders address.

84
00:02:22,809 --> 00:02:27,758
Bob sees his orders. The server looked up the token, and knew he is Bob, a customer.

85
00:02:28,308 --> 00:02:29,551
Now authorization,

86
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:32,243
which means what you are allowed to do.

87
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:33,969
Can Bob delete a product?

88
00:02:33,969 --> 00:02:34,728
Type: curl,

89
00:02:34,728 --> 00:02:35,350
dash s i,

90
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:36,385
dash capital X,

91
00:02:36,385 --> 00:02:36,868
delete,

92
00:02:36,868 --> 00:02:38,594
the authorization header,

93
00:02:38,594 --> 00:02:40,458
the address of product one,

94
00:02:40,458 --> 00:02:40,803
pipe,

95
00:02:40,803 --> 00:02:41,148
head,

96
00:02:41,148 --> 00:02:41,770
dash one.

97
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:44,729
Four oh three.

98
00:02:44,729 --> 00:02:45,378
Forbidden.

99
00:02:45,378 --> 00:02:47,780
The server knows exactly who you are,

100
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:49,533
and the answer is still no.

101
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:51,350
Your role does not allow it.

102
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:53,232
Now log in as Alice,

103
00:02:53,232 --> 00:02:53,897
the admin,

104
00:02:53,897 --> 00:02:55,162
and keep her token.

105
00:02:55,162 --> 00:02:56,693
Same command as before,

106
00:02:56,693 --> 00:02:58,556
with Alice and her password.

107
00:03:04,106 --> 00:03:07,498
Now try the delete again, as Alice. Delete product two.

108
00:03:12,848 --> 00:03:18,096
Two oh four. Deleted. Same request, different token, different role, different answer.

109
00:03:18,646 --> 00:03:20,001
Remember the difference.

110
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:22,203
Four oh one: I do not know who you are.

111
00:03:22,203 --> 00:03:24,122
Four oh three: I know who you are,

112
00:03:24,122 --> 00:03:25,025
and you may not.

113
00:03:27,075 --> 00:03:30,190
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

114
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:33,147
Alice's password lives in a file,

115
00:03:33,147 --> 00:03:34,315
not in the code.

116
00:03:34,315 --> 00:03:36,869
List the file with its permissions.

117
00:03:36,869 --> 00:03:37,598
Type: L S,

118
00:03:37,598 --> 00:03:38,109
dash L,

119
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:39,422
secrets dot E N V.

120
00:03:39,972 --> 00:03:41,920
Look at the start: dash R W,

121
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:42,406
dash R,

122
00:03:42,406 --> 00:03:42,893
dash R.

123
00:03:42,893 --> 00:03:45,536
Everyone on this computer can read it.

124
00:03:45,536 --> 00:03:47,761
Anyone could read the passwords.

125
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:48,804
That is a leak.

126
00:03:49,354 --> 00:03:54,944
Make it private to its owner. Type: C H mod, six hundred, secrets dot E N V.

127
00:03:55,494 --> 00:03:58,912
Six hundred means: the owner can read and write,

128
00:03:58,912 --> 00:04:01,191
and nobody else can do anything.

129
00:04:01,191 --> 00:04:02,260
Check it again.

130
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:02,972
Type: L S,

131
00:04:02,972 --> 00:04:03,470
dash L,

132
00:04:03,470 --> 00:04:04,752
secrets dot E N V.

133
00:04:05,302 --> 00:04:07,704
Now only dash R W at the start.

134
00:04:07,704 --> 00:04:08,324
Private.

135
00:04:08,324 --> 00:04:11,887
Last check: is a password written in the code?

136
00:04:11,887 --> 00:04:13,901
Search for Bob's password,

137
00:04:13,901 --> 00:04:15,605
and count the matches.

138
00:04:15,605 --> 00:04:16,458
Type: grep,

139
00:04:16,458 --> 00:04:16,999
dash C,

140
00:04:16,999 --> 00:04:18,239
paper dash kite,

141
00:04:18,239 --> 00:04:19,169
API dot P Y.

142
00:04:19,719 --> 00:04:20,577
Zero matches.

143
00:04:20,577 --> 00:04:23,546
The code only names the environment variable.

144
00:04:23,546 --> 00:04:26,251
The password itself is never in the code,

145
00:04:26,251 --> 00:04:28,231
so it can never end up in git.

146
00:04:28,781 --> 00:04:32,795
The rules: keep secrets in the environment or a private file.

147
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:33,980
Never in the code.

148
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:34,835
Never in git.

149
00:04:34,835 --> 00:04:36,546
And never in a screenshot.

150
00:04:38,596 --> 00:04:40,281
Let us put the ideas together.

151
00:04:40,831 --> 00:04:43,038
Authentication answers: who are you?

152
00:04:43,038 --> 00:04:43,712
You log in,

153
00:04:43,712 --> 00:04:45,918
and the server checks your password.

154
00:04:45,918 --> 00:04:47,573
A failure is a four oh one.

155
00:04:48,123 --> 00:04:50,437
Authorization answers: what may you do?

156
00:04:50,437 --> 00:04:52,098
The server checks your role.

157
00:04:52,098 --> 00:04:53,819
A failure is a four oh three.

158
00:04:54,369 --> 00:04:58,465
After log in, the server needs to remember you. There are two common ways.

159
00:04:59,015 --> 00:05:01,452
A session: the server keeps a record of you,

160
00:05:01,452 --> 00:05:03,612
and gives you a cookie with its number.

161
00:05:03,612 --> 00:05:04,775
The server remembers.

162
00:05:05,325 --> 00:05:07,920
A token: you carry the proof yourself,

163
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:08,739
in a header,

164
00:05:08,739 --> 00:05:09,900
on every request.

165
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:11,676
This is what our API does.

166
00:05:11,676 --> 00:05:14,339
The token can be a plain random string,

167
00:05:14,339 --> 00:05:15,432
or a signed one,

168
00:05:15,432 --> 00:05:16,525
such as a J W T.

169
00:05:17,075 --> 00:05:17,779
Either way,

170
00:05:17,779 --> 00:05:20,339
a token is a password with a short life.

171
00:05:20,339 --> 00:05:21,619
Protect it like one,

172
00:05:21,619 --> 00:05:22,771
and let it expire.

173
00:05:23,321 --> 00:05:24,453
In real systems,

174
00:05:24,453 --> 00:05:25,585
add more: M F A,

175
00:05:25,585 --> 00:05:27,071
and hashed passwords.

176
00:05:27,071 --> 00:05:30,467
But the pattern you saw today is the foundation.

177
00:05:32,517 --> 00:05:35,290
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

